  "strategy": "Expert and efficient step function search algorithm:\n\n1. Approach and Rationale:\n   Create a step function optimization system combining:\n   - Fourier-based acceleration (O(n log n) convolution computations)\n   - Directional splitting of axes for localized search\n   - Step-adaptive simplex method with exponential weighting\n   - Implicit tree-based coordinates construction from low-dimensional templates\n\n   This approach addresses previous solution's high core loss by using Fourier-normalization in the search space. It parameterizes step functions as products of Fourier components, allowing efficient high-dimensional optimization without exhaustive scanning. We set the stage for termination by:\n   - Fixing the reference vector magnitude (normalized to sum 1)\n   - Balancing soft updates with hard acceptance checks\n   - Embedding direction-specific scaling to maintain non-negativity\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   Represent step function search in Fourier domain:\n     a(x) = IFFT(z) / ||IFFT(z)||\u2081\n     where z is a vector with non-negativity constraints\n\n   Division-aligned coefficients: For any z\u2209\u211d\u207f\u207a, define normalization operator:\n\n     normalize(z): projections onto positive axes to remaining simplex\n\n   The exponentiated gradient framework uses:\n\n     w_k \u2190 exp(-\u03b5 * (\u03b1 - 1) * d_k / ||d||)\n\n   with directional subgradients defined by:\n\n     d_k = max_k(idx) - max_{alternate}(idx)  [for full convolution]\n\n   and the decision vector update:\n\n     z \u2190 z + \u03b1 \u2299 (random_direction)   [where \u2299 is Hadamard product]\n\n   This formulation preserves the problem's separability and allows piecewise optimization.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   Five-stage sequence optimization pipeline:\n\n   A. Initialization:\n      Start with a 3-term reciprocal vector: \n        base = [1, 1/2, 1/3] then generalized to length 384\n        complex_val = [np.exp(-2j\u03c0k/n) for k in 0..n-1]\n\n   B.Fourier-normalization:\n      Project onto Fourier space, both forward and inverse transforms.\n\n   C.ExpGradientStrict:\n      Iterative refinement using exponential weighting:\n        for each iteration \u03b5:\n          step:\n            arrays = IFFT(complex_val)\n            peaks = identify top-3 non-negative points in full convolution\n            gradients = expgradient update based on peak decline\n\n   D.AutomatonTreeGeneration:\n      Construct full fourier representation:\n\n        depth-1: base harmonic sequence\n        depth-2: scaling by 1.3 (soft) and subset selections (hard)\n\n   E.StepwiseTerminationCheck:\n      Merge candidate evaluations every 300 generations and accept if:\n\n            score_new < best_score - 1e-8\n\n   with exception handling for:\n      - Finite checks (pseudoevaluation using dropout)\n      - FFT failures (handled by duplicate filtering)\n      - Expweight normalization (automatic)\n\n   The final candidate must be projected to valid sequence space with non-negative coefficients and sum\u22650.01, then length-adapted via Fourier resampling.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   Deploy exponential weighting hyperparameter sweep on fixed-length n=384. Use these adaptive parameters:\n\n        base: \u03b5=0.5, decay=0.9996, eta_min=0.001\n        progressive: fluctuate epsilon between 0.7 and 1.2\n        Fourier-initialization: with 8 different root patterns\n        reciprocal-gradient-checking: to avoid getting stuck\n\n   For each expweight iteration, incorporate these steps:\n\n        grid = [0.001, 0.003, 0.005] * [0.5, 0.7, 0.8] * [1.2, 1.1]   [eta_fraction, beta, gamma]\n\n        and random coordinate selection (O(24) subsets)\n\n   5. Robust Time Allocation:\n      Distribute 1800 seconds dynamically between:\n        - Primary expansion (70%)\n        - Coordinate pruning (15%)\n        - Hard evaluation (10%)\n\n      Use a gradient-derivative mechanism to end:\n\n          term = drop_lower_convolution + 3*expweight_magnitude\n\n      where any component exceeding 90% of the best becomes an extreme leaf.\n\n   6. Final Validation:\n      Save all candidates to disk as structured records (security check).\n\n      Run final objective using:\n          twice_convolution_{max} = convolve(candidate)[round(0.7*n)]\n\n      must validate with all coefficients \u22650 and max_convo < 2*n*max_b/(sum^2)\n\n   This implementation uses:\n        - NumPy for FFT computations\n        - SciPy for optimization framework\n        - Pure-Python statistical checks\n\n   to ensure no dependence on commercial libraries.",
